{
  "concept": "Bucket sort distributes n numbers uniformly drawn from [0,1) into n equal-width buckets using the index floor(n·x), keeps each bucket's list sorted by insertion, and then concatenates the buckets in index order to produce the sorted output.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel label '(a)' centered under the left column",
    "Panel label '(b)' centered under the right structure",
    "Italic header 'A' above the left column",
    "Left column: ten gray rectangular cells stacked vertically, row indices 1–10 printed to the left of each cell",
    "Contents of A top to bottom: .78, .17, .39, .26, .72, .94, .21, .12, .23, .68",
    "Italic header 'B' above the right column",
    "Right column: ten gray rectangular cells stacked vertically, indices 0–9 printed to the left of each cell",
    "Diagonal NIL slash drawn inside B[0], B[4], B[5], B[8] (empty buckets)",
    "Horizontal arrow leaving each nonempty B cell into the head of its linked list",
    "Linked-list records: two-field gray boxes, left field holds the value, right field holds either a pointer stub with arrow or a diagonal NIL slash",
    "Chain at B[1]: .12 → .17 → NIL",
    "Chain at B[2]: .21 → .23 → .26 → NIL",
    "Chain at B[3]: .39 → NIL",
    "Chain at B[6]: .68 → NIL",
    "Chain at B[7]: .72 → .78 → NIL",
    "Chain at B[9]: .94 → NIL",
    "Optional interval annotation next to each B index showing the half-open range [i/10, (i+1)/10)",
    "Highlight styling for the currently read A cell and the currently targeted B bucket",
    "Number-line strip (0 to 1) marked into ten equal bucket intervals, used by the boundary probe"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "bucketSortEditor",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That bucket sort is two decisions repeated n times — which bucket (floor(n·x)) and where inside that bucket's sorted list — and that getting either one wrong visibly corrupts the concatenated output",
      "label": "Write the distribution + insertion pass of bucket sort",
      "entry_point": "bucketSort(A, B)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "read(i)", "does": "returns A[i] (1-indexed) and highlights that cell in panel (a)" },
        { "op": "listLength(b)", "does": "number of nodes currently in bucket B[b]" },
        { "op": "nodeAt(b, k)", "does": "value stored in the k-th node (0-based) of bucket B[b]" },
        { "op": "insertAt(b, k, x)", "does": "splice a new node holding x in as the k-th node of B[b] — the ONLY way to mutate the structure" },
        { "op": "mark(b)", "does": "highlight bucket b and shade its interval [b/10, (b+1)/10) on the number line" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "attach a short annotation to the current frame" }
      ],
      "frame": "both panels: A with the cell just read highlighted, B with every chain redrawn, and the bucket/node touched by the last op flashed",
      "input": { "label": "Input array A", "default": ".78, .17, .39, .26, .72, .94, .21, .12, .23, .68" },
      "sample_code": "function bucketSort(A, B) {\n  const n = 10;\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n    const x = read(i);\n    const b = Math.floor(n * x);   // half-open bucket [b/10, (b+1)/10)\n    mark(b);\n    // walk the chain to find the sorted insertion point\n    let k = 0;\n    while (k < listLength(b) && nodeAt(b, k) < x) {\n      k++;\n    }\n    insertAt(b, k, x);\n    note('placed ' + x + ' at position ' + k + ' of bucket ' + b);\n  }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function bucketSort(A, B) {\n  const n = 10;\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n    const x = read(i);\n    // BUG 1: round() instead of floor() -- .78 lands in bucket 8, not 7\n    const b = Math.round(n * x);\n    mark(b);\n    // BUG 2: always appends at the tail, so a chain can come out unsorted (.17 before .12)\n    insertAt(b, listLength(b), x);\n  }\n}",
      "success_check": "Every value of A appears exactly once in B; each value x lies in bucket floor(10x); and reading each chain head-to-tail gives increasing values, so concatenating buckets 0..9 yields the fully sorted sequence."
    },
    {
      "id": "distributeStep",
      "type": "state",
      "teaches": "That the buckets fill one input element at a time, and that each new node is spliced into its chain rather than appended blindly",
      "label": "Distribute A one element at a time",
      "states": ["idle", "running", "done"],
      "default": "idle"
    },
    {
      "id": "probeValue",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That floor(10x) partitions [0,1) into half-open intervals — the left endpoint belongs to the bucket, the right endpoint belongs to the next one, which is why .94 sits in bucket 9 and both .12 and .17 sit in bucket 1",
      "label": "Probe value x → bucket floor(10x)",
      "range": [0, 0.999, 0.001],
      "default": 0.94
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The unsorted input",
      "narration": "Panel (a) holds ten numbers drawn from [0,1) in arbitrary order. Panel (b) starts as ten empty buckets, each the head of a linked list.",
      "state": { "distributeStep": "idle", "probeValue": 0.78 }
    },
    {
      "title": "One element, one bucket",
      "narration": "Press step: A[1] = .78 is read, floor(10 × .78) = 7 highlights bucket 7 and its interval [0.7, 0.8), and a new node is drawn on that chain.",
      "state": { "distributeStep": "running", "probeValue": 0.78 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Insertion keeps each chain sorted",
      "narration": "When .12 arrives after .17, it is spliced ahead of it rather than appended, so bucket 1 reads .12 → .17. Each chain stays sorted as it grows.",
      "state": { "distributeStep": "running", "probeValue": 0.12 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The half-open convention",
      "narration": "Drag the probe across a bucket boundary: at x = 0.9 the value jumps into bucket 9 and stays there all the way to 0.999, so .94 can never spill into a tenth bucket.",
      "state": { "distributeStep": "done", "probeValue": 0.94 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Concatenate in index order",
      "narration": "All ten values are distributed; reading buckets 0 through 9 head-to-tail gives .12 .17 .21 .23 .26 .39 .68 .72 .78 .94 — the sorted output, with no comparison between buckets ever needed.",
      "state": { "distributeStep": "done", "probeValue": 0.5 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The bucket index floor(10x) already encodes the coarse ordering, so sorting reduces to tidying up tiny lists — concatenating the buckets in index order needs no comparisons across buckets at all.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two panels side by side on white. Left panel: header italic 'A', a single vertical stack of ten light-gray bordered cells with black serif indices 1–10 to their left and values .78 .17 .39 .26 .72 .94 .21 .12 .23 .68 top to bottom; caption '(a)' below. Right panel: header italic 'B', a matching vertical stack of ten light-gray cells indexed 0–9; cells 0, 4, 5, 8 contain a thin diagonal slash (NIL). Nonempty cells emit a horizontal black arrow to the right into a chain of two-field gray records — left field value, right field either a short pointer stub feeding the next arrow or a diagonal NIL slash. Chains, aligned on the row of their bucket and extending rightward: 1 → .12, .17; 2 → .21, .23, .26; 3 → .39; 6 → .68; 7 → .72, .78; 9 → .94. Caption '(b)' below. Bucket 2's chain is the longest and defines the figure's right edge. Add a faint horizontal 0-to-1 number line beneath panel (b) divided into ten equal ticks for the boundary probe, with the active interval shaded. Use one accent color (e.g. amber) for the currently read A cell and a second (e.g. blue) for the targeted bucket and newly inserted node; keep everything else grayscale so the touched elements read instantly during replay."
}

